fe.append_section(S, '03', '## The new implicit contract: `normalizeRecipe` trusts its caller', """Before the change `normalizeRecipe` coerced everything (`String(item)`), so it could be called with anything. Now it maps `item.trim()` over the ingredients, which means it may only be called *behind* `validateRecipe`, which has already proven every ingredient is a non-empty string. Call it directly with a bad body and you get:

```js
> normalizeRecipe({ name: "Soup", ingredients: [42] })
TypeError: item.trim is not a function
```

That is not a bug; it is a contract: validation happens once, at the boundary, and normalization assumes validated input. The cost is that the contract is invisible in the code. Two ways to make it visible: a comment above `normalizeRecipe` naming the precondition (added in the fix pass), or a type signature in a TypeScript version. Exercise 1 will make you feel this when you change the ingredient shape.""", before='## How to reason through the code')
fe.rw('server/server.js', lambda t: t.replace('function normalizeRecipe(input) {', '// Precondition: input has passed validateRecipe (ingredients are non-empty strings).\nfunction normalizeRecipe(input) {', 1))
done.append('review#1 03 documents the validate-before-normalize contract and the TypeError; precondition comment added above normalizeRecipe')
